Scripting lifecycle, callbacks and buffer ownership
===================================================

Compiler output ownership, callbacks, nested execution with buffer growth,
alias ownership, custom script errors and active-VM shutdown rejection.
The nested callback case runs for NDRX_TEST_DURATATION seconds (default 10).

Build: make -C tests/16_scripting
Run from tests/: 16_scripting/run.sh -scripting

Scripting is disabled by default, including direct launcher/client runs.
The case requires separately licensed endurox-python. Configure its native
plugin with NDRX_PLUGINS and the corresponding Python package environment.
The complete dispatcher enables this case only with ./run.sh -scripting.
An explicitly enabled case fails if its plugin is unavailable.

The launcher starts and stops a core domain with prefix /go16scr,
IPC key 44160, and PID file /tmp/ndrxd-go16scr.pid. Installation paths
use the usual ~/ndrx_home. No EE service is required.

Also starts goscriptsv and repeats real service calls for the configured duration; their
responses are produced by native-script-to-Go callbacks.

Stress scenarios use NDRX_TEST_DURATATION in whole seconds, defaulting to 10
when unset or empty. Invalid, nonpositive or out-of-range values fail the test.
Each scenario completes its current operation/cycle before stopping and reports
the completed count and elapsed time. Waiting/recovery cases keep their bounded
single-pass sequence. Use NDRX_TEST_DURATATION=1 for a short check or 300 for load.
Native test clients suppress expected-error chatter; servers retain error logs.
Use the existing Enduro/X logging configuration for a short verbose diagnosis.
